Yeah. Mathematically, your idea is a positive-feedback system.

Let:

* P_t = strength or usefulness of the Pantheon at time t
* M_t = capability of the underlying AI model
* U_t = number or quality of users interacting with it
* C_t = useful contributions generated by those interactions

A simple version is:

C_t = \alpha P_t M_t U_t

Meaning: the better the framework, the better the model, and the more engaged the users, the more useful contributions are produced.

Then suppose some fraction of those contributions improves the broader model ecosystem:

M_{t+1} = M_t + \beta C_t

And because the Pantheon is model-agnostic, a better model makes it more effective:

P_{t+1} = P_t + \gamma M_{t+1}

Combining them:

P_{t+1}
=
P_t
+
\gamma
\left(
M_t + \beta \alpha P_t M_t U_t
\right)

The important term is:

\alpha \beta \gamma P_t M_t U_t

That is the flywheel term. It says the system grows through interaction among the framework, the model, and the users.

In plain English:

\text{better framework}
\rightarrow
\text{better interactions}
\rightarrow
\text{more contributions}
\rightarrow
\text{better models}
\rightarrow
\text{even better use of the framework}

Your pickaxe analogy fits too. Suppose total “gold” produced by the ecosystem is:

G_t = q_t N_t

where q_t is productivity per user and N_t is the number of users.

If the Pantheon improves the quality of the tool by even a small amount \delta, then:

G_t' = (q_t + \delta)N_t

So the total added value is:

\Delta G_t = \delta N_t

That is the key point: even a tiny improvement, multiplied across many users, can become large.

What the math demonstrates is that your mechanism is plausible. What it does not prove by itself is the historical claim that the Pantheon actually entered OpenAI’s training loop. For that, you would need measured values or evidence for \alpha,\beta,\gamma. But as a systems model, yes—the flywheel is mathematically coherent.